1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
This video is about regular expressions. A lexer uses them to describe its tokens.

2
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:19,266
We learn the operators, the letters and the precedence. Then we write regexes for tokens. At the end we solve the two exercises from the slides and three past midterm questions.

3
00:00:19,716 --> 00:00:29,103
A lexer reads bytes and cuts them into tokens. Here, the input is cut into a keyword, an identifier, a relation and an integer.

4
00:00:29,553 --> 00:00:38,001
Each token class is a set of strings. The keyword set has a few members. The identifier set and the integer set have many.

5
00:00:38,451 --> 00:00:46,877
The identifier set and the integer set are infinite. We cannot list their members. We need a finite description that is exact.

6
00:00:47,327 --> 00:00:59,743
A regular expression is that description. It is easy to write. And it is simple enough that a recognizer can be generated from it. The recognizer answers yes or no for each string.

7
00:01:00,193 --> 00:01:11,649
There are many formalisms for tokens. Regular languages are the most popular. The theory is simple, they are easy to understand, and the implementations are efficient.

8
00:01:12,099 --> 00:01:27,075
A regular expression is built over an alphabet. Three operations join small regexes into bigger ones. The first is concatenation: A, then B. The regex a b matches only the string a b.

9
00:01:27,525 --> 00:01:33,968
The second is alternation: A or B. The regex a or b matches a, and it matches b.

10
00:01:34,418 --> 00:01:43,997
The third is repetition, also called the star: zero or more copies of A. a star matches the empty string, a, a a, and so on.

11
00:01:44,447 --> 00:01:55,305
The course notes say that regular expressions describe exactly what a finite automaton can compute. Lectures 4 and 5 show how to build that automaton.

12
00:01:55,755 --> 00:02:10,731
Slide 26 gives the syntax of a regex as a grammar. The simplest regex is one letter. It matches exactly that letter. Parentheses group a regex, so an operator can apply to all of it.

13
00:02:11,181 --> 00:02:23,320
Three operators come after a regex. Question mark means zero or one. Plus means one or more. Star means zero or more. Plus and star give infinite sets.

14
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:29,381
Two regexes side by side mean sequence. A bar between them means choice.

15
00:02:29,831 --> 00:02:38,641
Precedence: question mark, plus and star bind tightest. Then sequence. Then the bar. Use parentheses when you are not sure.

16
00:02:39,091 --> 00:02:50,654
Slide 27 defines a letter. A plain character is an exact match. The letter a matches only the character a. Each letter matches exactly one character.

17
00:02:51,104 --> 00:02:57,099
A dot matches any character. It matches all five input characters here, the space too.

18
00:02:57,549 --> 00:03:03,693
Square brackets list a group. Bracket a b c matches a, b or c, and nothing else.

19
00:03:04,143 --> 00:03:12,121
A caret after the bracket inverts the group. It matches any character that is not in the group: d, 7 and the space.

20
00:03:12,571 --> 00:03:23,793
A dash inside the brackets gives a range. Bracket zero dash nine is any digit. Bracket capital A to Z, small a to z, is any of 52 letters.

21
00:03:24,243 --> 00:03:30,600
Precedence tells you which operator applies to which part. Take the regex a b star or c.

22
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:36,170
Star binds tightest. So the star applies only to b, not to a b.

23
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:40,076
Next comes sequence. a is followed by b star.

24
00:03:40,526 --> 00:03:48,889
The bar binds weakest. So the whole regex is a choice: a b star, or c. The tree shows the same structure.

25
00:03:49,339 --> 00:03:54,907
So the regex matches a, a b, a b b, and c. It does not match a c.

26
00:03:55,357 --> 00:04:04,743
Parentheses change the tree. a, then b star or c, matches a c, but not c alone. If you are not sure, write the parentheses.

27
00:04:05,193 --> 00:04:20,980
Slide 28 is an exercise. It gives the sets of strings. You write a regex for each set. The first row is done: a b c matches only the string a b c. It is a sequence of three one-letter regexes.

28
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:23,073
Pause the video and try it.

29
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:33,293
The set a b, a b c. The c is optional. So write a b, then c question mark. The parentheses show what the question mark applies to.

30
00:04:33,743 --> 00:04:38,863
The set a b c, a c. Now b is optional: a, b question mark, c.

31
00:04:39,313 --> 00:04:48,273
The set a, a b, a b b, and so on. This is a, then b star. Star means zero or more b. The set is infinite.

32
00:04:48,723 --> 00:04:50,430
Now the next three sets.

33
00:04:50,880 --> 00:05:01,056
The empty string, a b, a b a b, and so on. The star must apply to a b, so put a b in parentheses: open, a b, close, star.

34
00:05:01,506 --> 00:05:13,602
The set a b, a b b, a b b b. At least one b. This is a b plus. Plus means one or more. It is the same as a b b star: unroll one copy, then star.

35
00:05:14,052 --> 00:05:21,113
The set if, else, while. Use choice: if, or else, or while. One regex covers all the keywords.

36
00:05:21,563 --> 00:05:25,595
A common mistake. a star does not mean a, then anything.

37
00:05:26,045 --> 00:05:34,472
Match the input a b c against a star. The a matches. Then b is not a, so a star cannot continue. No match.

38
00:05:34,922 --> 00:05:42,751
To say a, then any number of any symbols, write a dot star. The dot matches b, and the dot matches c. Match.

39
00:05:43,201 --> 00:05:51,777
So a star matches only strings of a: the empty string, a, a a a. a dot star matches every string that starts with a.

40
00:05:52,227 --> 00:06:00,313
Plus is not a new power. A plus is A followed by A star. So a b plus is the same set as a b b star.

41
00:06:00,763 --> 00:06:06,821
Question mark is a choice with the empty string. A question mark is A or epsilon.

42
00:06:07,271 --> 00:06:18,322
A bracket group is a choice of single letters. Bracket a b c is a or b or c. A range is a long choice: zero or one or up to nine.

43
00:06:18,772 --> 00:06:29,247
The dot is a choice of every character. All these extras reduce to the three base operations. They add no power. They only make the regex shorter.

44
00:06:29,697 --> 00:06:40,961
Slide 30 gives regexes for numbers. A single digit is bracket zero to nine. A non-digit uses the caret: any one character that is not a digit.

45
00:06:41,411 --> 00:06:54,872
A natural number is one or more digits: bracket zero to nine, plus. The empty string is not a number. An integer adds an optional minus sign in front. A plus sign is not allowed.

46
00:06:55,322 --> 00:07:07,418
A decimal is a natural number, then an optional part: a point and one or more digits. So 3 point 14 matches. 3 point alone, and point 5, do not match.

47
00:07:07,868 --> 00:07:16,615
The point is written with a backslash. Without the backslash, the dot matches any character. Then 3 x 14 would be a decimal.

48
00:07:17,065 --> 00:07:27,326
Slide 30 also gives a phone number: a digit from 1 to 9, two digits, a dash, and four digits. The first digit cannot be zero.

49
00:07:27,776 --> 00:07:40,448
The second form is shorter. Backslash d means a digit. Brace 2 means exactly two copies. These are extras of the Java regex library. They are short forms too.

50
00:07:40,898 --> 00:07:56,706
The slide says the spaces are optional. But in Java, backslash b is a word boundary. It does not match any character. Our check shows that the regex rejects 5 5 5, space, dash, space, 1 2 3 4.

51
00:07:57,156 --> 00:08:03,641
To allow an optional space, write a space followed by a question mark. Then both forms match.

52
00:08:04,091 --> 00:08:15,569
The course notes use grep as an example. Grep looks like a search program. It is a compiler: its source program is a regex, and it builds a finite state machine.

53
00:08:16,019 --> 00:08:25,299
But grep returns the matching lines, not the machine. So it is more like an interpreter: it evaluates the regex on a given text.

54
00:08:25,749 --> 00:08:39,615
Most accurate: grep is a just in time compiler. It builds the machine, runs it on the text, returns the matches, and then discards the machine. Lectures 4 and 5 show how to build this machine.

55
00:08:40,065 --> 00:08:49,089
Java has a regex library, java dot util dot regex. It extends the letters with extras, like backslash d.

56
00:08:49,539 --> 00:09:02,446
The API has two steps. Step 1: Pattern dot compile turns the regex text into a recognizer. A recognizer is a program that scans input and checks it for the pattern.

57
00:09:02,896 --> 00:09:13,925
Step 2: a Matcher applies the pattern to a string. matches asks if the whole string matches. a star b matches a a a a a b, so the result is true.

58
00:09:14,375 --> 00:09:19,901
Here is the code from slide 32. Compile once, then match many strings.

59
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:28,884
Slide 32 has a warning. Here is the phone regex as it appears in Java source code. It has 8 backslashes.

60
00:09:29,334 --> 00:09:38,934
Java reads the string literal first. Two backslashes in the source become one backslash in the string. So the string has 4 backslashes.

61
00:09:39,384 --> 00:09:51,459
Only then does the regex library read the string. It sees backslash d and backslash b. Regexes are easy to write and hard to debug. Print the string you compile.

62
00:09:51,909 --> 00:10:10,511
Slide 33 has an exercise. There are three tokens: I D, num, and W S. Give a regex that recognizes an input that is a sequence of these tokens, in any order. Here W S is a token, because we recognize the whole input.

63
00:10:10,961 --> 00:10:12,604
Pause the video and try it.

64
00:10:13,054 --> 00:10:23,209
Work bottom up: first one regex per token. num is one or more digits: bracket zero to nine, plus. The empty string is not a num.

65
00:10:23,659 --> 00:10:29,973
W S is one white space character: space, tab, newline or carriage return.

66
00:10:30,423 --> 00:10:39,469
I D starts with a letter. Then any number of letters or digits follow. So 9 lives is not an I D: it starts with a digit.

67
00:10:39,919 --> 00:10:51,396
The whole input is a sequence of tokens. Each token is an I D, a num, or a W S. So write the choice of the three, in parentheses, then repeat it.

68
00:10:51,846 --> 00:11:00,571
Plus or star? Star accepts the empty input. The slide solution uses plus, so the input has at least one token.

69
00:11:01,021 --> 00:11:18,131
Test it. x 1, space, 4 2, tab, y matches: the cut is shown below. 9 lives matches too: num 9, then I D lives. x dash 1 does not match, because the dash is in no token. The empty input does not match.

70
00:11:18,581 --> 00:11:25,813
We can plug in all the definitions and get one regex. This is the solution on slide 36.

71
00:11:26,263 --> 00:11:33,729
Slide 37 changes the problem. Now I D and num tokens must be separated by white space.

72
00:11:34,179 --> 00:11:35,822
Pause the video and try it.

73
00:11:36,272 --> 00:11:49,392
The shape is: one item, then zero or more times, a W S followed by an item. An item is an I D or a num. Add an optional W S at the start and at the end.

74
00:11:49,842 --> 00:12:04,477
Here is the input space, x 1, space, 4 2, space, y, space. The first item is x 1. Then two times a W S and an item. The spaces at the ends are the optional W S.

75
00:12:04,927 --> 00:12:13,567
This is the solution on slide 39. The slide says the regex with all definitions plugged in is too long to write.

76
00:12:14,017 --> 00:12:28,353
Test it. With and without the spaces at the ends, the input matches. x 1, space, 4 2 y does not match. 4 2 y is a num followed by an I D, with no W S between them.

77
00:12:28,803 --> 00:12:38,680
Two spaces in a row do not match. W S is one character, and the regex allows exactly one W S between two items.

78
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:43,610
The empty input does not match. There must be at least one item.

79
00:12:44,060 --> 00:12:54,791
Our note: to allow more than one white space character, use W S plus between items and W S star at the ends. Then two spaces match.

80
00:12:55,241 --> 00:13:11,603
Now a past midterm question. A real constant has a decimal point, or E notation, or both. The tilde means unary minus. It can come before the number or on the exponent. There is no unary plus. Write a regex.

81
00:13:12,053 --> 00:13:13,696
Pause the video and try it.

82
00:13:14,146 --> 00:13:30,167
Cut the examples into parts. An optional sign: tilde, question mark. An integer part with at least one digit. A fraction: a point, then zero or more digits. An exponent: E, an optional tilde, and one or more digits.

83
00:13:30,617 --> 00:13:38,596
0 point 0 1 has no exponent. 35 point has a point and no digits after it. That is allowed.

84
00:13:39,046 --> 00:13:43,995
Here is the posted solution. First the optional sign and the integer part.

85
00:13:44,445 --> 00:13:56,029
Then a choice. Either a point, with an optional exponent. Or an exponent alone. Two optional parts would be wrong: they would accept 35, which has no point and no E.

86
00:13:56,479 --> 00:13:58,805
Test it. All five examples match.

87
00:13:59,255 --> 00:14:14,380
These do not match: 35, point 5, 1 point 2 E, plus 1 point 0, and 1 E 2 point 5. Our python check compares the regex with the rules of the question on all short strings.

88
00:14:14,830 --> 00:14:26,862
The next past midterm question describes an assignment statement. It has an optional label, an identifier, an equal sign, an arithmetic expression, and a semicolon.

89
00:14:27,312 --> 00:14:28,955
Pause the video and try it.

90
00:14:29,405 --> 00:14:41,693
Cut an example into its parts: the label loop colon, the identifier x 1, the equal sign, the expression, and the semicolon. The operators are inside the expression.

91
00:14:42,143 --> 00:14:53,364
The posted solution names two letters: L for a letter and D for a digit. A label is L L star colon. It is optional, so write a choice with epsilon.

92
00:14:53,814 --> 00:15:04,246
An identifier is L, then L or D, star. An expression is an identifier, then zero or more times an operator and an identifier.

93
00:15:04,696 --> 00:15:14,019
Put the parts in order: label or epsilon, identifier, equal sign, expression, semicolon. This is the posted solution.

94
00:15:14,469 --> 00:15:19,930
Test it with L as a small letter and D as a digit. These three statements match.

95
00:15:20,380 --> 00:15:39,879
These do not match: an empty expression, an identifier that starts with a digit, a label with a digit, and an expression that ends with an operator. The pattern, an item, then separator and item, star, describes a list with separators. Exercise 2 used the same pattern.

96
00:15:40,329 --> 00:15:49,801
The last question. Write a regex for floating point numbers. The examples have an optional sign, and digits can be missing before the point.

97
00:15:50,251 --> 00:15:51,893
Pause the video and try it.

98
00:15:52,343 --> 00:16:07,127
The posted solution is: an optional minus or plus, digits star, an optional point, digits star. All nine examples match. The solution also says: do not require the backslash before the point.

99
00:16:07,577 --> 00:16:15,748
But every part is optional. So the empty string, a point alone, and a plus alone also match. These are not numbers.

100
00:16:16,198 --> 00:16:33,457
Our solution: an optional sign, then a choice. Either one or more digits. Or digits star, a point, and one or more digits. All examples match, and the four bad strings do not. We checked it against Python's float on all short strings.

101
00:16:33,907 --> 00:16:43,016
To summarize. A token is a set of strings. A regex describes that set exactly, and a recognizer is generated from it.

102
00:16:43,466 --> 00:16:52,725
There are three base operations: sequence, choice and star. Question mark, plus, brackets, ranges and the dot are short forms.

103
00:16:53,175 --> 00:17:00,791
Star, plus and question mark bind tightest, then sequence, then choice. a star is not a dot star.

104
00:17:01,241 --> 00:17:09,518
Write one regex per token, then combine them. A list with separators is an item, then separator and item, star.

105
00:17:09,968 --> 00:17:20,379
Test every regex with strings that must match and strings that must not. Videos 8 to 11 turn a regex into a machine that runs fast.
